      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© Mary Ann Liebert, Inc.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "This study aimed at evaluating the effect of massage therapy on the pulmonary functions of stable Egyptian children with asthma."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "This study was an open, randomized, controlled trial."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTINGS/LOCATION",
            "content": "The study was conducted in pediatric allergy and chest unit of the New Children's Hospital of Cairo University, Egypt."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SUBJECTS AND INTERVENTIONS",
            "content": "Sixty (60) children with asthma were divided randomly into two equal groups: massage therapy group and control group. Subjects in the massage therapy group received a 20-minute massage therapy by their parents at home before bedtime every night for 5 weeks in addition to the standard asthma treatment. The control group received the standard asthma treatment alone for 5 weeks."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "OUTCOME MEASURES",
            "content": "Spirometry was performed for all children on the first and last days of the study. Forced expiratory flow in first second (FEV1), forced vital capacity (FVC), FEV1/FVC and peak expiratory flow (PEF) were recorded."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "At the end of the study, mean FEV1 of the massage therapy group was significantly higher than controls (2.3-0.8 L versus 1.9-0.9 L, p=0.04). There was no significant difference in FVC (2.5-0.8 L versus 2.7-0.7 L, p=0.43). However, FEV1/FVC ratio showed a significant improvement in the massage therapy group (92.3-21.5 versus 69.5-17, p<0.01). PEF difference was not significant (263.5-39.6 L/minute versus 245.9-32 L/minute, p=0.06)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "A beneficial role for massage therapy in pediatric asthma is suggested. It improved the key pulmonary functions of the children, namely, FEV1 and FEV1/FVC ratio. However, further research on a larger scale is warranted."
          }
        ]
      },
